00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:12.150 align:start position:0%
 
hi  this is lena lee global commercial  product manager for the visel product  line  welcome to the short presentation on  transferring cell counting methods  recommended steps to matching results  there are many reasons that drive  adoption of a new  method it can be obsolescence of  equipment or materials  you might need to increase your  throughput or  you might need to decrease the variation  in your current process  or just general process improvements  in our example uh we recently released a  new instrument called the by cell blue  so when customers are transferring their  current method to the visel blue they  may need to  perform bridge studies or correlation  studies in order to migrate  their existing method to the new  instrument  in this presentation i will provide an  example of  a step-by-step approach on migrating  a vice lxr to the visyl blue  in getting started it's important to  first verify  the performance of the cell counting  methods to be evaluated  there's a protocol outlined by nist  called evaluating the quality of a cell  counting measurement process  in this paper they outline how you can  use  a dilution series to assess instrument  performance  in addition to using cells you also  want to utilize standardized  beads for concentration viability  and also size these are  independent of cells so they they give  you  a better baseline indication of  instrument  performance without the variability  that you may get from live cells  you can also leverage um a reference  cell counting method such  as a culture counter like the  multi-sizer  or z-series or even hemocytometer  but hemocytometer adds another level of  variability  but in in all together it's very  important to verify  the counting performance of your method  before you get started with optimizing  and doing your correlation studies  and here we will begin showing you  examples of how  we were able to match cell counts  between  a new cell counting analyzer called the  visel blue  and its predecessor which is called the  bicel  xr both models are fully automated  cell viability cell counting analyzers  they both utilize the tripan blue dye  exclusion  method  [Music]  the vicel blue is our next generation  cell counter  from the visel xr the viso blue was  designed  with the flexibility to adjust the cell  type settings  to correlate or match with an equivalent  sample  that was also run on the vice lxr  this was very important in our  development of the visel blue  we want to note that complete matching  may not always be possible  in some cases cell samples may have  higher variability  this can be due to either lower  concentration samples  or lower viability or clumpy  cell lines these can add a higher level  of  variation but in general it should be  possible to match  the performance between your instruments  or methods  to get an acceptable level of matching  here we will show you a step-by-step  process  that we used to match the bicel blue and  the visel xr  using typical cell type parameters for a  representative cell sample  in our testing we were able to  achieve concentration results that were  within 10 percent of each other  and viability within 5 percent  the first step in our method for  matching cell type parameters  was to ensure that the counting  performance  for the viso xr and vicel blue was  verified  using their recommended standard beads  and protocols  after we verified that both instrument  passed  their concentration control standards we  then moved on to  run a common concentration control  sample  on both systems so the same sample  was run on both instruments  in this case we use the visel xr  concentration control which is about a 1  million  beat per ml sample  alternatively you could use different  bead concentration standards  as long as their concentration were  determined using a particle counter such  as a  multisizer culture counter and  we want to verify that  both of the instruments using this  common concentration control  did present a result that was within 10  percent of each other  this helps us get some confidence that  both instruments are in good counting  uh accuracy um  and so if they're further apart than 10  of each other we would want to  troubleshoot a little bit deeper  to understand where that difference is  before we proceeded  once we're sure that both instruments  are counting within 10  of each other we then can begin to run  live cells  and here we recommend to use  cell concentrations that are greater  than 2 million  so you have a good representative count  and greater than 50 viability  so there's not too much debris in your  samples  and when you are running these samples  you want to run the same sample  side by side on both instruments so  they're run  as close as the same time to each other  just to decrease that variability  we also suggest to run samples in  replicate  you know perhaps triplicate would be  good  this will help to improve the  statistical confidence  use the appropriate cell type for  measuring the cell sample  and then save the images this way you  can reanalyze the data without having to  rerun the samples  in our testing method we use the cho  cell line we ran the same samples on  three of iso blues and three visol xrs  using their appropriate cell types  our goal was to match the systems within  10  of each other for concentration and 5  for viability  you can definitely determine your own  degree of matching but these are  typically as as tight as you would want  to go if you  uh want to have a tighter  correlation which is definitely possible  but  not always realistic just because of the  tolerance  stack ups between the process overall  of counting sample prep the samples  themselves  you know can add the variability that  you know you  sometimes cannot get any any closer  than 10 for concentration and 5 for  viability  here i just want to give an overview of  what cell types  are here is an example  of parameters in a cell type from the  visel blue  the cell type is a collection of  settings that control the sample  preparation and analysis parameters  the cell type includes the number of  images  to use in analysis the sample prep  settings which are the number of mixes  that you might  have for your sample also the cell count  threshold and the viability threshold  this table shows the cell type settings  that were used  to do our correlation study  these are settings for the vyso blue  which was the mammalian cell type and  the chose cell type  for the bicel xr  with the cell type settings that i just  showed in the previous slide  we were able to demonstrate  that we could optimize the cell types on  the visel blue  to correlate the concentration results  to be within  percent of the bicel xr and  viability within 1.1 percent and  and the average diameter of the cells  were also less than 2 percent difference  from each other  so to just wrap up our recommendations  are that you utilize concentration of  viability standards  as part of your study use cell samples  that are greater than 2 million  cells per ml in concentration with  higher than 70 percent  viability you also want to practice good  sample handling  your samples side by side so they're run  as close in the same time practice good  statistics you know run samples in  replicas  and you want to account for variables in  the process  you know set realistic tolerances and  you should be able to determine this  based on your  counting verification counting  performance  that you would have done before you  started your  correlation studies with the two methods  and then definitely account for  differences in technology  um in our example the visol xr is an  instrument that was  out on the market close to  19 20 years ago versus the vyso blue  which has  a very recent technology so you will  have some differences in the technology  that can add to  the differences within the two two  methods  and for more resources you can always go  to beckman.com  we have a page with all the different  cell counters and analyzers  our multi-sizer as well as our visel  blue vyso xr  and also the viscel metaflex if you were  interested in media analysis  there's also a page on our website that  has cell type  guidelines to help you with cell type  optimization  thank you for your time if you have any  questions please feel free to  contact me at lena lee beckmann.com

